Print the stack bounds when the stack check fails

The check compares sp against round_page(kstackptr) - KERNEL_STACK_SIZE.  Print
sp, the thread's kstackptr, the computed bottom and KERNEL_STACK_SIZE so it is
clear whether the recorded stack top is wrong or the size constant does not
match a 4 KiB page port.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
index 2cc442e..c2ea058 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
@@ -2082,6 +2082,9 @@ sleh_invalid_stack(arm_context_t *context, uint32_t esr __unused, vm_offset_t fa
 
 	sp = get_saved_state_sp(&context->ss);
 	kernel_stack_bottom = round_page(thread->machine.kstackptr) - KERNEL_STACK_SIZE;
+	printf("nio: badstack sp %p kstackptr %p bottom %p size %#x esr %#x far %#x\n",
+	    (void *)sp, (void *)thread->machine.kstackptr,
+	    (void *)kernel_stack_bottom, KERNEL_STACK_SIZE, esr, far);
 
 	if ((sp < kernel_stack_bottom) && (sp >= (kernel_stack_bottom - PAGE_SIZE))) {
 		panic_with_thread_kernel_state("Invalid kernel stack pointer (probable overflow).", &context->ss);
